Kent Paul Dolan wrote:
> On Apr 8, 10:15 am, "John H." <Joh...@gmail.com> wrote:
> 
>>I think Nethack scoring should be based primarily on:
>>- Player wealth

I don't want to comment on whether it should be the primary factor, but I
have the feeling that it should be a more essential part of the scoring
than it is currently.

> I totally disagree. Wealth is a _tool_ in NetHack, it
> would distort the game entirely to make it a _goal_.

I disagree.  As long as you can read in the introductory chapters of the
Nethack Guidebook

       "You have just begun a game of NetHack.  Your goal is to grab
   as much treasure as you can, retrieve the Amulet of  Yendor,  and
   escape the Mazes of Menace alive."

there's the impression that it would be important as one of the primary
goals to get as much wealth as possible.

> Many, many commenters here recommend dumping gold as a
> worthless nuisance past the midgame.

As do I suggest.

> Your game would be much less fun to play than one with
> scoring based on demonstrated skill rather than based
> on demonstrated carrying capacity. Do we reward most
> highly those who can nest bags of holding most deeply?
> That's what "wealth as the main score component" would
> end up entailing.

The bag of holding example is extreem; at least in my whole Nethack life
there was just once the situation (recently) that I got all the necessary
bags, and the reward was nothing. OTOH, it's also a heavy trade-off; you
still risk blowing it up.

> Why go there?

Generally, if you decide to carry more wealth you restrict yourself from
carrying more useful objects that you need. It's a trade-off; you should
decide whether to go for some more score points or increase survivability.

Janis


